    {
      "duration_ms": 12000,
      "caption": "Every guest page-table pointer is itself a GPA that needs its own EPT walk.",
      "narration": "The hard part is that guest page tables hold guest physical addresses. Each of the four guest levels must be translated through the EPT before it can even be read.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Why it nests",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "n",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Guest L4 entry: a GPA, walk EPT",
            "Guest L3 entry: a GPA, walk EPT",
            "Guest L2, L1: same again",
            "Final data GPA: walk EPT once more"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 20.0,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 600,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "color": "orange"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 11500,
      "caption": "Worst case with 4 levels each: 24 memory references for one TLB miss.",
      "narration": "With four guest levels and four EPT levels, each guest step costs four EPT reads plus its own read. Five steps of five, minus one, is twenty-four references.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "The cost",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "m",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "text": "(4 + 1) * (4 + 1) - 1 = 24",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 20.0,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 600,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Native 4-level walk: 4 references.",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 35.9,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 1900,
          "enter": "fade",
          "color": "gray"
        },
        {
          "id": "hl-m",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "m",
          "at": 3600
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 11500,
      "caption": "TLBs cache the combined GVA to HPA result; huge pages shorten both walks.",
      "narration": "In practice, the TLB caches the final combined translation, and paging-structure caches skip upper levels. Backing guests with 2 MiB or 1 GiB pages shortens the EPT walk.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Taming the walk",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "t",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "TLB holds GVA -> HPA directly",
            "Paging-structure caches skip levels",
            "2M/1G EPT pages: fewer levels"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 20.0,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 600,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "color": "green"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 12500,
      "caption": "A missing EPT entry raises an EPT violation exit; the host fills it lazily.",
      "narration": "If the EPT has no mapping, the CPU takes an EPT violation exit. KVM's MMU allocates or faults in the host page and resumes. The same trap mechanism powers dirty logging.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "EPT violations",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
